{
  "title": "MPs Deny Rejecting Animal Sentience Despite Contentious Brexit Vote",
  "summary": "A recent vote on the EU withdrawal bill has sparked outrage and confusion, with MPs scrambling to clarify that the UK still recognizes animal sentience despite rejecting an amendment to enshrine EU law on the matter.",
  "story": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "MPs have been forced to deny that a Commons vote last week means animals are not recognised in the UK as sentient life forms. The controversy stems from an amendment to the European Union (withdrawal bill) aimed at transferring the EU protocol on animal sentience into UK law. The amendment was defeated by 313 votes to 295 on November 15th, and since then, a heated debate has erupted regarding the true implications of the vote."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In an attempt to quell the uproar, Tory MP Zac Goldsmith described some of the reporting on the vote as “weird and dishonest,” clarifying via Twitter that the vote concerned the correct legal procedure for upholding animal welfare standards, not the fundamental principle of animal sentience itself. Fellow Conservative Rachel Maclean, MP for Redditch country, posted a similar video, decrying “fake news” and asserting that, “This government, and in fact all governments, are deeply committed to continuing to protect animals as sentient beings. That law is already written into our own law.”"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Prime Minister Theresa May addressed the issue during Wednesday’s PMQs, stating unequivocally: “We recognise and respect that animals are sentient beings, and should be treated accordingly.” Reinforcing this message, Tory MP Hugo Swire republished a letter from Environment Secretary Michael Gove, adding: “I do not believe that any MP thinks that animals do not have feelings or emotions.”"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Despite these assurances, the vote has ignited widespread protests. Animal rights groups have launched multiple online petitions targeting the House of Lords, UK parliament, and Michael Gove. Celebrities like Sue Perkins have voiced strong criticism of MPs' actions."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Gudrun Ravetz, senior vice-president of the British Veterinary Association, expressed serious concern, stating: “It is extremely concerning that a marginal majority of MPs have voted-down this seminal clause. This action undermines the government’s previous promises that the UK will continue to be known for our high standards of animal health and welfare post-Brexit.”"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "However, Conservative MP Henry Smith argued that EU law still permits many practices across Europe that are opposed by animal rights groups. He suggested that focusing solely on transposing Article 13 might not be the most effective way to improve animal welfare standards."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The amendment, spearheaded by Green party co-leader Caroline Lucas, aimed to incorporate Article 13 of Title II of the Lisbon Treaty into UK law. During the debate, Lucas highlighted that the origins of the clause dated back to the UK's presidency of the EU in 1997 and that EU law has explicitly recognized animal sentience since 1999. The debate underscores the complex relationship between Brexit, legal procedure, and animal welfare in the UK."
    }
  ]
}
```

